Write the event to the [[g:same database in the same transaction]] as the business data, then use a separate relay to publish it to the broker.

<pre><code>@Transactional
void placeOrder(Order order) {
    orderRepository.save(order);
    outboxRepository.save(new OutboxEvent("OrderCreated", order.getId(), toJson(order), PENDING));
    // commit together or roll back together — atomic because both writes use the same DB
}</code></pre>

- [[b:Polling]]: `SELECT ... WHERE status='PENDING' FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets multiple relay instances run in parallel without contending for the same rows. It is simple and often sufficient
- [[b:CDC]]: Debezium reads the transaction log (binlog/WAL) and publishes changes to Kafka. It offers low latency, adds no polling-query load, and preserves ordering, at the cost of more infrastructure to operate

:::deep
`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets N relays scan the same table while skipping rows another relay has locked → horizontal scaling without double-publishing through concurrent selection.

The Outbox pattern [[o:guarantees at-least-once, not exactly-once]] delivery. A crash before marking an event SENT causes it to be sent again, so the consumer [[r:must be idempotent]] (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).
:::

Publisher confirms acknowledge publisher-to-broker acceptance according to the queue type and durability configuration.

Three common application patterns are synchronous per message, synchronous batches, and asynchronous callbacks; they are patterns with different throughput/failure bookkeeping, not three different broker guarantees.

<pre><code>channel.<span class="f">confirmSelect</span>();
ConcurrentNavigableMap&lt;Long, Message&gt; outstanding = <span class="k">new</span> ConcurrentSkipListMap&lt;&gt;();
channel.<span class="f">addConfirmListener</span>(
    (seqNo, multiple) -&gt; {
        <span class="k">if</span> (multiple) outstanding.<span class="f">headMap</span>(seqNo, <span class="k">true</span>).<span class="f">clear</span>();
        <span class="k">else</span> outstanding.<span class="f">remove</span>(seqNo);
    },
    (seqNo, multiple) -&gt; retryQueue.<span class="f">addAll</span>(<span class="f">removeNacked</span>(outstanding, seqNo, multiple)));
<span class="c">// register before publish; remove/resolve the entry if basicPublish itself throws</span>
<span class="k">long</span> seq = channel.<span class="f">getNextPublishSeqNo</span>();
outstanding.<span class="f">put</span>(seq, message);
channel.<span class="f">basicPublish</span>(exchange, key, properties, body);</code></pre>

`multiple=true` acknowledges or rejects the prefix through `seqNo`.

A skip-list map makes ordered concurrent prefix lookup practical, but clearing `k` entries is [[r:not O(log n)]]: locating the range is logarithmic while visiting/removing the acknowledged entries costs at least O(k) work (and implementation overhead per removal).

:::deep
A confirm is not an end-to-end delivery or processing acknowledgement. Durability depends on queue type, replication policy, durable declarations, persistent-message properties, and broker failure semantics; consumer acknowledgements remain separate.

Test node loss, channel close, returned unroutable messages, and connection recovery.

Asynchronous mode must handle single and cumulative ack/nack, channel shutdown with unresolved entries, bounded outstanding memory, mandatory-return routing failures, retry backoff, and duplicate publication.

Keep callback work small; hand retries to a controlled executor/queue and do not assume channel operations are freely thread-safe.

Confirms also do not solve the database→broker dual write. Use a transactional outbox for that boundary (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7), then treat confirm state as relay bookkeeping.
:::

The client sends an [[g:idempotency key]], typically a UUID representing one unique operation intent:

<pre><code>@PostMapping("/transfers")
Response transfer(@RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key, @RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    boolean firstTime = idempotencyStore.tryInsert(key, IN_PROGRESS);   // atomic
    if (!firstTime) {
        var rec = idempotencyStore.get(key);
        if (rec.status == COMPLETED)   return rec.savedResponse;         // return the original result
        if (rec.status == IN_PROGRESS) return Response.status(409);      // still processing
    }
    Response resp = doTransfer(req);
    idempotencyStore.update(key, COMPLETED, resp);                       // store the ENTIRE response
    return resp;
}</code></pre>

- [[o:Store the entire response]] because a retry needs the original transaction ID, not merely an empty 200 OK
- use an [[o:atomic CAS]] such as `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` backed by a unique constraint, never SELECT-then-INSERT, which races
- handle [[b:IN_PROGRESS]] by returning 409 so the client waits. Keep the TTL long enough to cover the legitimate retry window

:::deep
[[r:The trap]]: `doTransfer` completes, but the process crashes before `update` → the key remains IN_PROGRESS forever.

Put a timeout on IN_PROGRESS and make `doTransfer` idempotent at the next layer; ideally, include both operations in the same database transaction. Also use a [[g:natural key]] such as transferId as a database unique constraint → two independent safety nets.
:::

For an internal transfer between two accounts owned by the same ledger, keep the money invariant inside [[g:one ACID double-entry transaction]]. Do not model the debit and credit as separate saga steps: a crash between them would temporarily create or destroy money.

<pre><code>1. Validate POST /transfers and bind Idempotency-Key to tenant + normalized request hash.
2. In one ledger DB transaction:
     - atomically claim the idempotency/business transfer ID;
     - lock/version the affected accounts in a deterministic order;
     - append balanced immutable debit and credit entries;
     - write transfer status and an outbox event;
     - store the replayable API result, then commit.
3. Outbox relay publishes TransferPosted; consumers deduplicate by eventId.
4. Notification/search/analytics update asynchronously and may lag without changing ledger truth.
5. Apply deadlines, concurrency limits, retry, breaker, or fallback only where each downstream's
   failure semantics require them; do not wrap every call with every resilience decorator.
6. Trace the flow without putting credentials/PII in trace baggage, and continuously reconcile
   ledger balance, transfer state, outbox, and external statements.</code></pre>

If the destination is an external bank/PSP, the local transaction records a balanced internal obligation/pending state plus outbox.

A durable orchestrator then performs the external step with a stable provider idempotency key, handles timeout as an [[o:unknown outcome]], polls/webhooks/reconciles, and records a compensating or forward recovery entry.

It never edits immutable ledger history to pretend the attempt did not occur.

:::deep
The correctness chain is explicit:

- payment idempotency and ambiguous outcomes: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1) and (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2);
- balanced ledger model: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3);
- outbox atomicity: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3);
- idempotent consumer: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6);
- network deadline/retry controls: (15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q5);
- trace and messaging causality: (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2).

Crash-test every boundary: before/after DB commit, before/after broker confirmation, duplicate/out-of-order delivery, provider success followed by local timeout, process kill during recovery, and stale read.

For each, state the durable source of truth and the mechanism that converges without double effect.
:::
